I started this thread on the AlphaNT list and I was just wondering if
there are any x86 assembly Gurus around who can verify if this is
possible or not. I have no intention nor do I have the skill to do such
a thing but if it could be done it would be a big reason to stray from
intel and go to Alpha or AMD.

<message>

<snip>
> The decision to pull the spots comes after privacy issues were raised about
> the Pentium III, which includes technology that allows Internet Web sites to
> identify visitors' computers.

I saw the coolest thing in Assembly class today. My prof. explained this
method though not the specifics of tricking the Intel processor into
thinking it has rebooted so it could change  modes or something. He also
told us that all of the Intel chips including todays still have the
instruction code in 'em back from the 8086 for backwards compatibility
(Why?) and that you could get to those instructions  if you wanted to.
Then a light bulb in my head went off :)

Intel claims that you have to reboot the computer to disable/enable 
this privacy thingee. Soooo given the facts I previously stated it is
theoritcly possible to trick the processor that it has rebooted and let
you enable/disable the privacy thingee. Now if a second year E.E student
could figure out this form of attack ( Not that I even have the skills
or care to do that sort of thing). What could a real cracker do if they
set their mind to it? Now how secure is Intel's idea? just another
reason to stick with Alpha. 

</message>
