On Fri, 12 Feb 1999, Thatcher Hubbard wrote:

>   I'm overwhelmed at the amount of people who had suggestions on this one,
> thanks all.  After thinking for a minute I decided that I could confirm my
> overheating theory more easily than most others.  So I took the top of the
> case off and set it in front of a small box fan.  It's loud, and it's
> causing slight jitters on the 19" monitor for my PC right now, but it's been
> up for 18 hours and 53 minutes according to 'top'.
>   At any rate, thanks again to the list members for turning out to help a
> new Alpha owner.

	This disscussion maybe a dead horse by now, and further
disscussion may only be flogging it further, but here are my two cents on
the matter.
	About a year ago my UDB started blacking out and refusing to
restart for 30 or so minutes. I did two things to fix it:

	Opened up the case, and cleaned the power contacts, especially the
ones connected to the green wires. They provide the main chip voltage for
the entire motherboard from what I have been told. I actually end up
taking the power connector from the power supply apart and cleaning the
contacts down inside the connector. They were quite black, and the
connector housing around the green wires was no longer white, but a
discolored orange. :)

	Took two 24V fans and connected them up to an external supply
(~14V), and attached them with electrical tape to the top of the UDB when
it is sitting on its side. They blow up, sucking air through the system,
and blowing hot air up and out. I later built out of wood a frame that
fits over the top of the UDB and has the fans screwed onto them. 

	After these modifications, it has been rock solid. My two cents.

